  "content": {
    "id": "p10-q20-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "20",
    "prompt": "An arithmetic sequence of positive integers has first term $a$ and common difference $d$. Given that the sum of the first 16 terms is 1000, determine the possible values of the pair $a$ and $d$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(a,d)=(10,7),(25,5),(40,3),(55,1)$.\n\nUse $2a+15d=125$ and the positive-integer restrictions.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}S_{16}&=\\frac{16}{2}[2a+(16-1)d]\\\\S_{16}&=8(2a+15d)\\end{align*}$$\nHence,\n$$\\begin{align*}8(2a+15d)&=1000\\\\2a+15d&=125\\\\a&=\\frac{125-15d}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nSince every term is a positive integer, $a\\ge1$ and $d$ is a non-negative integer. The equation requires $d$ to be odd, and $a\\ge1$ gives $15d\\le123$. Thus $d=1,3,5,7$.\n\nSubstitution gives $(a,d)=(55,1),(40,3),(25,5),(10,7)$. Each pair satisfies $S_{16}=1000$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
